     "malleable",
     "ductile",
     "rusting"
    ],
    "text": {
     "en": "Most metals react with oxygen to form oxides and with acids to give hydrogen. Iron rusts when it meets air and water. Gold and silver do not react easily, so they stay shiny.\nMetals conduct heat and electricity and can be beaten into sheets (malleable) or drawn into wires (ductile).\nNon-metals like sulphur, carbon and oxygen are brittle and poor conductors, except graphite."
    }
